<Docs slug="" description="How to install osewa, keep sessions on a host, forward ports, run the server as a service, and manage device keys.">
<p class="lede">osewa keeps your terminal sessions on the host and reaches them over QUIC, or a TLS WebSocket where UDP is blocked. You use ssh once, to install it and enroll your device. After that the shell outlives the network.</p>

<h2>The pieces</h2>
<p>There is one binary, <code>osewa</code>. The same file plays every part:</p>
<dl>
  <dt>the client</dt>
  <dd>On your laptop: <code>osewa login</code>, <code>attach</code>, <code>forward</code>, <code>exec</code> and the rest. It holds your device key and the list of hosts you have logged in to.</dd>
  <dt>the server</dt>
  <dd>On the host: <code>osewa server</code> listens on port 7322, TCP for the TLS WebSocket and UDP for QUIC. It checks each device's key and relays its traffic to sessions. Usually it runs as a user service.</dd>
  <dt>the holders</dt>
  <dd>One process per session, started by the server. A holder owns the shell's PTY and keeps the real screen in libghostty-vt, Ghostty's terminal engine. Holders don't depend on the server: restart or update the server and every session keeps running. (After an update to an incompatible build, older sessions run on but can't be attached.)</dd>
</dl>

<h2>What happens when you connect</h2>
<ol>
  <li>The client dials QUIC and the TLS WebSocket together, giving QUIC a 300 ms head start, and keeps whichever connects first.</li>
  <li>Both ends prove who they are with Ed25519 keys over TLS 1.3. The host's key was pinned when you enrolled, so there is no "trust this host?" prompt.</li>
  <li>The host sends the session's current screen as one snapshot, then streams changes.</li>
  <li>If the network drops, the client notices within about a second, redials, and resumes. Keystrokes are numbered and resent until the host acknowledges them, so none are lost or typed twice.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Platforms</h2>
<p>Hosts run Linux or macOS, and so does the command-line client. There is no Windows build. <a href="https://getmoshi.app">Moshi</a>, the terminal app for <a href="https://apps.apple.com/app/id6757859949">iOS</a> and <a href="https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=app.getmoshi.android">Android</a>, has osewa as an experimental transport beside mosh. The host needs osewa and moshi-hook.</p>

